Nan Wang <nwang@panix.com> writes:

> Given that Elbereth is an elven goddess, how come engraving it does not
> break the atheist conduct?

How do you imagine the protection provided by Elbereth to work?  If
it's a direct call to the goddess to protect you, then it violates
atheism, but it could also be an attempt to take advantage of the
monsters' faith in her, in which case an atheist would have no
problem with it.

Unfortunately, neither of those explanations is quite consistent.
Engraving Elbereth works in Gehennom, where no god can help you, and
on animals and mindless creatures who presumably cannot read and do
not know of the elven goddess.  So I suppose it's just magic (and does
not violate the atheist conduct, but does violate the hypothetical
no-magic conduct).
